{
  "dom_id": "step-q277832",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[4]{x-2}}+1.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[4]{x-2}}+1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[4]{y-2}}+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[4]{y-2}}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{y-2} & =\\frac{3}{x-1} \\\\ (\\sqrt[4]{y-2})^{4} & ={(\\frac{3}{x-1})}^{4} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{81}{(x-1)^{4}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{81}{(x-1)^{4}}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{81}{(x-1)^{4}}+2.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>2,\\,f(x)>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
